{"id":"8b8a411b-4659-49d5-b396-28e5b324666c","object":"chat.completion","created":1785030030,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    nodes = case['nodes']\\n    core = case['core_distance']\\n    distances = case['distances']\\n    parent = {node: node for node in nodes}\\n    rank = {node: 0 for node in nodes}\\n    def find(x):\\n        while parent[x] != x:\\n            parent[x] = parent[parent[x]]\\n            x = parent[x]\\n        return x\\n    def union(x,y):\\n        rx, ry = find(x), find(y)\\n        if rx == ry: return False\\n        if rank[rx] < rank[ry]:\\n            parent[rx] = ry\\n        elif rank[rx] > rank[ry]:\\n            parent[ry] = rx\\n        else:\\n            parent[ry] = rx\\n            rank[rx] += 1\\n        return True\\n    edges = []\\n    for u,v,d in distances:\\n        w = max(core[u], core[v], d)\\n        edges.append((w, u, v))\\n    edges.sort(key=lambda x: x[0])\\n    mst_edges = []\\n    total_weight = 0.0\\n    for w,u,v in edges:\\n        if union(u,v):\\n            mst_edges.append([u, v, w])\\n            total_weight += w\\n    return {'mst_edges': mst_edges, 'total_weight': total_weight}\"}","reasoning_content":"We need to create Python code for solve(case) that computes the Minimum Spanning Tree (MST) of a graph defined by nodes, core_distance, and distances. The output must have mst_edges (list of [node1, node2, weight]) and total_weight (sum of weights). The graph is constructed using mutual reachability distance: d_mr(a,b) = max(core_distance[a], core_distance[b], distance(a,b)). Then we need to find the MST of the complete graph (or at least the graph from distances list, but distances list includes all pairs? Actually distances list has all pairwise distances given. But based on the problem, it seems the input distances are all pairs, so complete graph. We need to compute MST using e.g., Kruskal's algorithm. Use standard library only.\n\nImplementation: parse nodes list; build list of edges with mutual reachability distance. Then sort edges by weight. Use Union-Find (disjoint set) to select edges for MST. Return mst_edges list and total_weight. Keep weights as floats.\n\nEdge case: there might be multiple edges with same weight; any valid MST is fine.\n\nReturn as a dict with keys \"mst_edges\" and \"total_weight\". Ensure JSON serializable.\n\nWrite code.\n\nNote: The fixture payload includes _case_nonce, but that's irrelevant.\n\nWe'll implement UnionFind class.\n\nMake sure to compute mutual reachability distance correctly: max(core_dist[a], core_dist[b], distance from distances list). The distances list contains triples [node1, node2, distance]. We'll create a dictionary for quick lookup.\n\nAlternatively iterate over distances list.\n\nImplementation steps:\n\n- n = len(case[\"nodes\"])\n- core = case[\"core_distance\"]\n- edges = []\n- For each triple (u,v,d) in case[\"distances\"]:\n    weight = max(core[u], core[v], d)\n    edges.append((weight, u, v))\n- Sort edges by weight.\n- UnionFind.\n- mst_edges = []\n- total = 0.0\n- For w, u, v in sorted edges:\n    if find(u) != find(v):\n        union(u,v)\n        mst_edges.append([u,v,w])\n        total += w\n- Return {\"mst_edges\": mst_edges, \"total_weight\": total}\n\nMake sure to return mst_edges as list of lists, not tuples, because JSON expects arrays.\n\nNow write the Python source as a string. In JSON, escape newlines and quotes.\n\nWe must output exactly one JSON object with key \"implementation\". The value is the Python source code as a string.\n\nLet's produce the code.\n\nWrite:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    nodes = case['nodes']\n    core = case['core_distance']\n    distances = case['distances']\n    parent = {node: node for node in nodes}\n    rank = {node: 0 for node in nodes}\n    def find(x):\n        while parent[x] != x:\n            parent[x] = parent[parent[x]]\n            x = parent[x]\n        return x\n    def union(x,y):\n        rx, ry = find(x), find(y)\n        if rx == ry: return False\n        if rank[rx] < rank[ry]:\n            parent[rx] = ry\n        elif rank[rx] > rank[ry]:\n            parent[ry] = rx\n        else:\n            parent[ry] = rx\n            rank[rx] += 1\n        return True\n    edges = []\n    for u,v,d in distances:\n        w = max(core[u], core[v], d)\n        edges.append((w, u, v))\n    edges.sort(key=lambda x: x[0])\n    mst_edges = []\n    total_weight = 0.0\n    for w,u,v in edges:\n        if union(u,v):\n            mst_edges.append([u, v, w])\n            total_weight += w\n    return {\"mst_edges\": mst_edges, \"total_weight\": total_weight}\n\nThat's fine.\n\nNow produce JSON output. Ensure proper escaping.\n\nThe entire response must be exactly one JSON object. So:\n\n{\"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    nodes = case['nodes']\\n    core = case['core_distance']\\n    distances = case['distances']\\n    parent = {node: node for node in nodes}\\n    rank = {node: 0 for node in nodes}\\n    def find(x):\\n        while parent[x] != x:\\n            parent[x] = parent[parent[x]]\\n            x = parent[x]\\n        return x\\n    def union(x,y):\\n        rx, ry = find(x), find(y)\\n        if rx == ry: return False\\n        if rank[rx] < rank[ry]:\\n            parent[rx] = ry\\n        elif rank[rx] > rank[ry]:\\n            parent[ry] = rx\\n        else:\\n            parent[ry] = rx\\n            rank[rx] += 1\\n        return True\\n    edges = []\\n    for u,v,d in distances:\\n        w = max(core[u], core[v], d)\\n        edges.append((w, u, v))\\n    edges.sort(key=lambda x: x[0])\\n    mst_edges = []\\n    total_weight = 0.0\\n    for w,u,v in edges:\\n        if union(u,v):\\n            mst_edges.append([u, v, w])\\n            total_weight += w\\n    return {'mst_edges': mst_edges, 'total_weight': total_weight}\"}\n\nMake sure to escape double quotes inside the string? The string